{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# S6 — Bounded concurrent gate executions, with fast-fail admission\n\nImplements the decision headed **\"Bounded concurrency with FAST-FAIL admission (S6)\"** in the\nLANDED `docs/adr/0112-on-box-disk-ceiling-architecture.md` on `origin/main`. Decisions are cited\nby HEADING, never number: prerequisite 7dfa records an earlier draft with a shorter Decision\nlist, so its numbers do not match the merged file.\n\n## Why\n\nS2–S5 cap each accumulator with a high-water-mark GC, and **a watermark GC assumes evictable\ndata exists**. The janitor relies on POSIX delete-on-last-close and takes no per-reader lease,\nso it may `unlink` an in-flight snapshot but the blocks return only when the last reader closes.\nUnder the operator's **~10 parallel plan reviews** the held bytes are LIVE, not garbage: the GC\nfinds nothing evictable and the volume fills anyway. Bounded concurrency is a **precondition**\nfor S2–S5. Both gates copy the repo at a ref, so they share **ONE counter** (two caps of N each\nadmit 2N); at capacity the call **fast-fails**, since a queued gate holds its thread, ~739 MB\nresident, and the client's request past its deadline.\n\n## Design\n\n**Seam.** `peak_rss.gate_peak_rss` already wraps this scope and says why: wrapping\n`review_plan()` / `verify_completion()` \"covers both call paths at one seam, since both reach\nthe gate through these functions\". `gate_admission.gate_admission` takes that seam but sits\nstrictly OUTSIDE it — `gate_peak_rss` wraps the run, admission wraps handle resolution AND the\nrun, since `gate_source.resolve_gate_handle` materializes the snapshot and spends the bytes. It\nbecomes the outermost `with` in each entry point, with the existing\n`handle = gate_source.resolve_gate_handle(...)` line moved inside it, and adds a second term to\nthe existing pre-clone admission point (`ensure_min_free_space`), not a parallel mechanism.\n\n**Counter.** N slot files in the store's existing `locks/` dir, each held for the run under a\nnon-blocking `fcntl.flock(LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB)`, released in a `finally`. Cross-process (AC4) and\ncrash-safe (AC5: the kernel drops a flock when the holder dies — no heartbeat, which the janitor\nrejected as unsound). It serialises across threads of one process too, since a flock is held by\nthe open file description and each acquisition opens its own; and it inherits\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR`, following gate scratch onto the ADR's dedicated volume.\n\n**Fail-open, but ANNOUNCED.** If admission's own plumbing is unusable (no `fcntl`, an unwritable\nstore) the gate is ADMITTED: refusing would turn one unwritable directory into a total gate\noutage, while admitting only overshoots concurrency with the free-space floor and janitor still\napplying — the posture `has_min_free_space` takes on `OSError`. But a silently disarmed cap is\nhow bug 3276 recurs, so each such admission emits one `GATE_ADMISSION_DISARMED {json}` marker\nnaming the reason.\n\n**Refusal — the hierarchy decides the mapping.** `GateCongestedError` lives in\n`src/rebar/llm/errors.py` as a **direct subclass of `LLMError`** (not `LLMRunnerError`: no\nrunner ran), and its `__init__` attaches\n`.outcome = LLMOutcome(ResolutionClass.WAIT_AND_RETRY, diagnostic, retryable=True)`. Those two\nfacts fire the mapping through arms that ALREADY exist, so **no new `except` clause is added**:\n\n- MCP: `_review_plan_body` / `_verify_completion_body` catch `rebar.llm.LLMError` →\n  `_structured_llm_failure`, which reads `outcome_of(exc)` (`getattr(exc, \"outcome\", None)`), so\n  the attached outcome yields `retryable: true` + `resolution_class: \"WAIT_AND_RETRY\"` (without\n  it, `false`). No `verdict` key — congestion is unreadable as INDETERMINATE, BLOCK or FAIL.\n- CLI: both handlers catch `llm.LLMError` → `_llm_error_exit_code`, returning **11** (\"transient\n  — retry\") exactly when `outcome.retryable`, else 1, after an `Error:` line naming congestion.\n- Vocabulary: `_errors._llm_error_code` gains an `isinstance(exc, GateCongestedError)` branch\n  returning a new `\"gate_congested\"` code in `KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`, placed **before every existing\n  arm** (the bare `isinstance(exc, LLMError)` would otherwise report it as `command_failed`).\n  The error-taxonomy contract test asserts the family is EXHAUSTIVELY mapped, so the subclass is\n  registered there too — that test verifies this in-session.\n\n**Metric (AC7).** One line-start `GATE_CONGESTED {json}` stderr marker per refusal, following\nthe `GATE_PEAK_RSS` convention: the containers hold no AWS credentials, so the host probe greps\njournald — and it is portable, working under the CLI with no CloudWatch or CI provider.\n\n**Config (AC6).** `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, resolved by `resolve_gate_max_concurrent`\nbeside `resolve_gate_min_free_bytes`. `0` disables the bound (the `max_bytes` idiom); a\nmalformed or negative value falls back to the default rather than disarming the cap. No\n`REBAR_GATE_*` env tier, deviating from the ADR's measured-defaults decision: a new `REBAR_*`\nliteral is a new `env_var` mechanism and bug `a1f1-a30d-2d50-4f5a` makes any new mechanism\nunlandable today, while `[snapshot]` keys are exempt (`_RESERVED_SECTIONS`, absent from\n`_SECTIONS`). One line to add once `a1f1` is fixed.\n\n**Default = 4, from measurement.** `GATE_PEAK_RSS`: plan_review ~748 MB, verify_completion\n~462 MB (the ADR cites ~739 MB / ~4.7 min and a degraded run at 501 MB, so it varies run to\nrun). The host is a `t4g.large` (8 GiB) shared with Gerrit and the ADR's host-sizing decision\nforbids growing it; ~2.17 GB steady state plus Gerrit's ~3 GiB reservation leave roughly 3 GiB\nfor gate work. 4 × 748 MB ≈ 2.99 GB fits; a fifth run (~3.74 GB) does not. 4 is well below the\nobserved ~10 concurrent — a cap at or above observed peak sheds no load.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: one counter bounds both gates. Hold `limit` slots as `plan_review`, assert a\n  `verify_completion` admission is refused.\n- [ ] AC2: at capacity the call fast-fails — the refused call returns in under one second while\n  a holder is still running.\n- [ ] AC3a: the refusal names host congestion and says to retry (both words in the message), and\n  is never an `LLM-Review −1` on the review-bot path (ADR 0069's retryable deferral).\n- [ ] AC3b: it is not readable as a verdict — the MCP body returns `retryable: true`,\n  `error == \"gate_congested\"`, and NO `verdict` key.\n- [ ] AC3c: the CLI returns exit 11 for a congestion refusal.\n- [ ] AC4: the bound spans processes — a real child holds every slot; this one is refused.\n- [ ] AC5a: a killed holder frees its slot — SIGKILL a holder, assert a later admission wins.\n- [ ] AC5b: a gate that raises frees its slot — raise inside the wrapper, assert the next\n  admission succeeds.\n- [ ] AC6a: the cap reads `[snapshot].max_concurrent_gates`, defaults to 4, treats 0 as off, and\n  falls back to the default on a malformed or negative value — one test per behaviour.\n- [ ] AC6b: `docs/config.md` AND `docs/plan-review-gate.md` both name the key, its default and\n  rationale, and the latter says a congestion refusal is an expected outcome, not a failure.\n- [ ] AC7: a rejected admission emits exactly one `GATE_CONGESTED` marker naming gate, ticket\n  and limit; an admitted one emits none.\n- [ ] AC8: a disarmed cap is announced — with admission's store unusable the gate is admitted\n  AND exactly one `GATE_ADMISSION_DISARMED` marker naming the reason is emitted.\n\n## Testing\n\n`pytest tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py -q` plus the error-taxonomy contract test, then\n`make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `pytest tests/unit/test_mechanism_delta.py -q` (not run by\n`make lint`; it is what catches a new mechanism name).\n\n## File impact\n\nNew: `llm/gate_admission.py`, `tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py`. Edited: `llm/errors.py`,\n`llm/plan_review/__init__.py`, `llm/completion.py`, `_config_resolvers.py`, `_errors.py`, the\nerror-taxonomy contract test, `docs/config.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`. Unchanged by design:\n`_mcp_llm.py`, `_cli/_llm_commands.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788494565982901001,"uuid":"28f475af-74e3-423d-87fe-576fa1513451"}